Durarara!! AU
Chapter 21 – Part 3 
Mei tried her luck again anyway.
She said it lighter the second time, as if repetition might turn a refusal into a game. “Come on. An address isn’t a confession. It’s just logistics. In case the conversation gets interesting after sushi.”
Curtis did not smile.
“No. You’re not getting my address.”
Mei put a hand to her cheek, delighted by the bluntness.
“So cold~”
Curtis’s mismatched eyes stayed on her, long black braids shifting against his uniform as the answer came out dry enough to end the bit.
“Like the final circle of hell.”
Shinra actually choked on a laugh.
“That’s ice, Mei. Ninth circle. He’s calling you a frozen lake, not a tease.” He looked far too pleased that the foreigner had literature ready at a sushi counter. “You see? Polite and abnormal. I told you.”
Mei’s mouth curved, unrepentant.
“A man who quotes damnation instead of giving out his unit number. Fine. I’ll live. For now.”
Celty’s private voice cut toward Curtis, low and fiercely satisfied.
Good. Hold that. She does not need a door into your building after the night she already left on someone else.
Curtis only reached for his tea, public face still even, the gift bag at his knee unmarked by the exchange. He had kept his cool. He had kept Simon’s shop intact. He had not given Mei a map to the suite.
Outside, Anri was still waiting on after.
Inside, the final circle of hell sat between him and a smiling doctor who now knew exactly how little thaw she was going to get.
The public table was still smiling when Curtis opened the private line.
Remember what you said to me the other night.
Celty went still beside him. Helmet on the counter. No face to give her away. The channel answered anyway, quieter than the jealousy she had just spent on Mei.
I know. Is it too late to take it back?
Curtis’s fingers tightened once against his knee.
Celty…
He did not get the rest of it out.
Mei leaned in with the exact timing of someone who could not hear the mind-link and still managed to walk straight through it. Her tone was professional enough to pass for inquiry if you wanted to be generous. It was not generous.
“While we’re on bodies and recovery,” she said, as if following a chart, “Shinra mentioned unusual durability, scar density, and a nervous system that doesn’t behave. From a functional standpoint, has that affected partnered physical response? Pain threshold during intimacy, delayed fatigue, anything statistically off from ordinary human sex. For comparison.”
Shinra made a small delighted sound that he poorly disguised as scientific interest.
The sushi counter did not get quieter. It just got worse.
Curtis turned his head toward Mei with the same flat cold he had used on the address, long black braids shifting against his uniform. The private line to Celty stayed open and unfinished, Celty… still hanging between them like a door Mei had just kicked a tray through.
Celty’s next thought came through sharp and furious.
She does not get that either.
Curtis set his tea down with care Simon would have approved of.
“That’s not a dinner question,” he said. “And it’s not a study.”
Mei only smiled, unrepentant behind the clipboard that did not exist.
“Everything is a study if the subject is interesting enough.”
Outside, the cigarette was already gone.
Inside, the other-night conversation had been interrupted at the exact word it should not have been.
And Curtis, who had promised himself he would not do anything in Simon and Denis’s store, sat on the edge of that promise while two conversations — one intimate, one disguised as science — occupied the same stool.
Curtis did not raise his voice.
He did not need to. Long black braids shifted against his uniform as he looked at Mei with the same cold that had named the ninth circle.
“I will not talk about subjects like that at Denis and Simon’s restaurant. It’s disrespectful.” A short, hard pause. “Have some manners. And at the very least, some shame.”
The words cut the disguised inquiry off at the root. Not I’m shy. Not ask later. A line drawn for the house he was sitting in.
Simon, from the counter, gave a low approving grunt that might have been coincidence and might not. Shinra’s delighted expression faltered into something almost sheepish, which on him still looked like interest wearing a weaker coat.
“Ah,” Shinra said. “Yes. Venue. Boundaries. Valid.” He cleared his throat. “Mei, perhaps the functional-response questionnaire can live somewhere that is not a public sushi bar.”
Mei held Curtis’s gaze a second longer, then sat back with her hands raised in a small concession that was not quite apology.
“Manners and shame,” she repeated. “That’s a very old-fashioned refusal. Fine. I’ll put the clipboard away before your friends behind the counter start treating me like a health-code violation.”
Celty’s private voice came through low and fiercely aligned with him.
Good. She does not get to turn this place into an exam room. And she does not get to finish the question she used to interrupt us.
Curtis left his tea where it was, public face still even, the unfinished Celty… still sitting in the mind-link like a conversation Mei had only delayed, not erased.
“Dinner,” he said. “That’s what this was supposed to be. Act like it.”
Curtis looked at his phone.
The later hour was already waiting on it. Anri. After. A night that did not belong to this counter. Long black braids shifted against his uniform as he stood, gift bag left behind as intended, wallet already in hand.
“I got places to be.” He nodded once to Shinra. “Now — Shinra, it was interesting as always.” Then, quieter, to the empty space above Celty’s collar: “Celty. See ya.” Last, to the woman who had asked for his address and his body under the cover of science: “Mei… well. We met.”
He paid everyone’s bill before Shinra could perform generosity and before Mei could turn a split check into another conversation. The register accepted it. The table had no time to argue the gesture into meaning more than it did: he had been invited, he had eaten enough of the evening, he was leaving it settled.
Simon lifted a huge hand from the counter.
“Curtis! Always good to have you. Have good day!”
Curtis tipped his head in answer, the faint smile from earlier finally visible enough to count, and walked out of Russia Sushi into the street.
Behind him, Shinra started talking at once, already trying to recapture the specimen.
Mei watched the door with interest that had not cooled.
Celty sat with an unfinished private line — Remember what you said to me the other night / Is it too late to take it back / Celty… — and the fact that he had said see ya like the rest of it could wait outside a restaurant she could not even eat in.
The fortune cookies had not come yet.
That would be another hour, another part of the night, after the boy with places to be had already gone to keep them.
The fortune cookies arrived after Curtis was already gone.
Simon set them down with the same booming generosity he gave everything else, three little sealed verdicts on a table that had just lost the person they were most honestly about. Shinra took them as dessert. Mei took one as a joke. Celty took one because Shinra put it in front of her, even though she could not eat the cookie, only the meaning.
She cracked hers first.
The slip of paper sat in her gloved fingers, plain and merciless.
A lost object will be found.
Shinra leaned over, already translating it into hope the way he had in every quieter version of this night.
“This means you’ll find what you’re looking for.”
Celty went still.
In canon that sentence pointed at a head. In this room it pointed at too many things at once: the stolen face she had crossed an ocean for, the unfinished private line Curtis had left hanging in the air — Remember what you said to me the other night — and the boy who had just paid the bill and walked out toward another hour that did not include her. A lost object. What she was looking for. The fortune did not specify which loss it intended to return.
Black smoke wanted to rise and had nowhere public to go. She typed nothing. She only kept the slip.
Shinra opened his own, read it, and performed the same small crime he always did.
Unrequited love.
He ate the paper before Celty could ask, then smiled with both hands slightly raised.
“Mine didn’t have one.”
Mei watched that lie happen with bright, lazy interest and only then opened hers. The slip made her mouth curve.
“‘What you reach for already belongs to someone else.’” She flicked the paper against her nail. “Rude. Accurate. And arriving one cigarette too late.”
Shinra laughed too quickly.
Celty sat with her canon fortune in her hand and the AU weight of it in her chest. She would find what she was looking for. That was supposed to be comfort. Instead it felt like a warning and a promise sharing the same sentence, because she no longer knew whether the lost object was her head…
or the man who had said Celty, see ya and left her in a restaurant she could not even eat in, with a doctor hiding unrequited love and a woman who had asked for his address like it was dessert.
There was a fourth cookie.
Simon had set it down with the others out of habit, one extra sealed slip for the seat Curtis had already emptied. It sat by the gift bag of tea like a message that had missed its owner. Mei’s hand moved toward it first — not even subtle about it, as if a leftover fortune were just another way to get a look at the boy who would not give her his address.
Celty took it before Mei’s fingers closed.
The motion was clean and final. Gloved hand over paper. No PDA delay. No public argument. Just the headless woman claiming the last object on the table that still belonged to him.
Mei’s eyebrows rose.
“Possessive again. It’s a cookie, Celty. Not his apartment.”
Celty did not give it back.
She cracked Curtis’s fortune herself, angling the slip so Mei could not steal the words by leaning in. Shinra, who had already swallowed unrequited love, watched with the uneasy brightness of a man realizing the dessert course had become territorial.
The paper in Celty’s hand was short.
What you leave behind will follow you anyway.
Celty went still.
A lost object would be found — that one was hers. This one was his, and it sounded too much like the night she already knew: drowned phone, open door, tower drop, restored number, Anri waiting after dinner, ruined shoes in another boy’s hands, a white scythe that could not be named. He could leave the restaurant. He could leave the table. He could leave a conversation hanging on Celty… and walk toward someone else.
The fortune did not care.
What he left behind would follow him anyway.
Mei, denied the slip, smiled without warmth.
“Well? Does the wilderness get a kinder future than I did, or did the cookie also tell him to stop running?”
Celty folded Curtis’s fortune once and put it away instead of answering. The private thought was already there, sharp and unwanted:
I am not giving this to her.
And I am not sure I should give it to him tonight either.
On the street, unaware he still had a cookie in someone else’s glove, Curtis was already heading toward the later hour he had promised Anri — leaving behind a table that had started finding lost things the moment he walked out the door.
Out on the street, Curtis opened his new phone and checked the time against Anri’s later hour. Long black braids shifted against his uniform as the leftover tea-and-interrogation heat of Russia Sushi thinned into ordinary evening.
“…Cool. I have a bit before I need to meet up with Anri.”
He walked.
The route did not stay simple. A car dealership spilled light across the sidewalk, rows of clean bodies behind glass, price boards doing the kind of math that made Ikebukuro look suddenly more permanent than a transfer student’s rented flat. Curtis’s steps slowed despite himself. For a second the old two-person future — we, not I — occupied the glass with him. A bike had already been an impulse. A car would be a life.
He shook his head once.
“No. If we spend more, we are screwed. We will have to work.”
The we left his mouth out of habit. There was no second duffle bag walking beside him to argue.
Justice argued anyway.
Curtis could not hear him. The street did not change. The dealership glass kept showing only one tall foreigner talking himself out of a purchase. But the brother’s voice arrived in the space the reader could occupy and Curtis could not, fond and exasperated and already finished with being dead as an excuse for frugality.
Idiot. You have my duffle bag of money. Don’t be afraid to spend it.
A dry, loving scoff.
So foolish. Why do you gotta be like this? You’re my brother. Money won’t do me any good anymore, Curtis.
Before Japan they had split the pile six ways: Curtis, Justice, and the four who still called themselves the Undead. Two of those bags had left the States in Curtis’s keeping. One was his. One was Justice’s. He had carried the second across an ocean like a relic and never opened it. Rent, tuition, the bike, the tea gift, Anri’s shoes — all of that had come from the bag he considered his own. The other one stayed untouched, as if spending a dead brother’s share would make the absence official.
Curtis kept walking.
He did not hear the permission. He only felt the old reflex: don’t burn the reserve, don’t live like the money is infinite, don’t build a life so expensive that work becomes a chain. The dealership lights slid off his uniform and back into the street. A car would have been easy. Touching Justice’s bag would have been the real crossing.
He checked the time again and turned toward the later hour with Anri, still poor in the one way that had nothing to do with cash:
he would rather go without than spend the half of the fortune that still felt like it belonged to someone who was supposed to be alive to argue about it.
Curtis found a tourist overlook that asked nothing of him except that he sit.
Ikebukuro spread out under the evening lights in the usual postcard version: crowds, signage, the city performing itself for people who still thought it was only scenery. He took a bench at the edge of it, long black braids shifting against his uniform, gift-giving and sushi-interrogation already behind him, Anri still ahead. For a few minutes he was just a tall foreigner killing time at a local spot, the dealership argument unfinished in his mouth and Justice’s unheard permission sitting in a duffle bag he would not touch.
His phone buzzed.
Celty.
The name alone put Russia Sushi back in his hand. The mind-link leftover. Remember what you said to me the other night. Is it too late to take it back. Celty… Then Mei’s clipboard question, the bill, see ya, and the door.
He opened the message.
Celty:
You left before dessert.
A second one followed almost immediately, as if she had argued with herself over how much to send.
Celty:
There was a cookie for you.
I took it before Mei could.
Then the line that was not about pastry.
Celty:
It said: what you leave behind will follow you anyway.
A pause in the thread. Then, lower, more like the private channel than a courier text:
Celty:
I still don’t know if it’s too late to take the other night back.
Don’t disappear into the rest of your evening without telling me you got this.
Curtis sat on the tourist bench with the city glittering in front of him and Celty’s fortune for him glowing in his palm — a lost object finding him after all, just not in the restaurant, and not in the form of a head.
Anri was still later.
The cookie had already arrived.
Curtis stared at the tourist lights for a second longer, then thumbed back a reply that was calmer than the dinner had been and more generous than the night she was trying to take back.
Curtis:
its fine celty. we were both upset that night.
Curtis:
its fine. you can take back what you said about the soft landing and all that jazz.
Curtis:
i already forgave you. i just didnt get a chance to tell you because mei is….well. something else.
Curtis:
even erika and walker would get uncomfortable with her
He sent the last one, then added a shorter line so the thread would not die on Mei alone.
Curtis:
got your message. i didn’t disappear. just had to leave before the cookies.
On the bench, long black braids shifted against his uniform as he locked the phone loosely in his hand. The fortune she had taken from Mei still sat in her glove somewhere across the ward: what you leave behind will follow you anyway. He had just told her she could take the other night back. He had also told her he had already forgiven her.
Those two things were not the same.
He knew that.
He sent them anyway, because Anri was still later, the city was still glittering like a postcard, and the version of him that exploded at sushi counters had been left outside Simon’s with the cigarette.
Curtis stared at the locked phone like the fortune might crawl out of it and explain itself.
Then he tipped his head back against the bench and muttered at the tourist skyline, long black braids shifting against his uniform.
“What the hell does that cookie mean?! That could mean Anri, Celty, or Rio!!! Fuckin’ vague!!!”
The city offered no footnote. What you leave behind will follow you anyway sat over all three names at once: the girl with his address and new shoes waiting on after, the headless woman holding his dessert slip and asking if the other night could be taken back, and the one who had already walked into his flat when the bottle was on the calendar. He had left pieces of himself with each of them in different shapes. The cookie had not specified which piece was doing the following.
From the cheap seats of the afterlife, Justice lost it.
The laugh was bright, unhelpful, and entirely at his brother’s expense — the sound of a dead boy watching the living one get ambushed by a restaurant slip instead of a bullet or a cryptid.
Oh, that’s beautiful. You wanted a prophecy and they handed you a group chat.
Curtis, who could not hear him, still scowled at the lights like someone nearby was enjoying this too much.
Justice kept laughing anyway.
Anri, Celty, or Rio. Brother, the cookie didn’t get vague. You did. You left a trail behind all three of them and now you’re mad the paper noticed.
On the bench, Curtis dragged a hand down his face.
“Useless cookie.”
Accurate cookie, Justice wheezed, unheard. You’re doomed in triplicate. I’d help, but this is the funniest thing I’ve seen since you said ‘yeah if you want’ and then cleaned the whole flat with panic smoke.
The day did not end in one place. It ended in four.

Mikado and Masaomi
Mikado still had the ruined shoes.
Not in his hands anymore — in a bag on the bench between them, like a verdict he refused to throw away. Masaomi sat beside him with his usual brightness worn down to something almost honest, watching his friend’s profile against the evening crowds.
“You’re going to keep that bag,” Masaomi said.
Mikado did not deny it.
“I found them.”
“I know.” Masaomi leaned back, eyes on the street rather than the wound. “And she has new ones. And she stopped calling you -kun. And he left school before the rest of us finished pretending it was still a normal day.” A short pause. “If you start thinking the only way to matter here is to become bigger than a pair of shoes, that’s when this city eats you.”
Mikado’s late smile tried to come back. It failed.
He stared at the bag and saw the doorway again: Curtis already finished being useful, Anri holding what could be worn, himself arriving with what could not. The notebook sentence sat under it. The version of this city where I was still necessary.
Masaomi saw the hunger take another quiet inch and hated that recognizing it did not make it smaller.

Anri
Anri had left them earlier on purpose.
The new Nikes were on her feet now. They felt too clean, too kind, too much like a scene she had handled badly and then tried to take back over the phone. She waited on a side street near the place Curtis had said he would come after dinner, phone in both hands, replay looping whether she wanted it or not: I can’t ignore you. Rio’s lecture. Stay away from him. The later hour she had claimed before anyone else could occupy it.
She told herself she was only making sure he was not alone.
That was true.
It was also not the whole truth.
Every extra minute he did not appear made Rio’s voice louder. Every extra minute also made Mikado’s ruined pair sit heavier in her memory, a kindness she had thanked second and a boy she had just started unnaming. When her phone stayed quiet, she did not text first. She had already asked him to come. She would wait. Waiting felt like proof she still had a place.

Russia Sushi
At the counter, the cookies had already done their damage.
Celty still had two slips: her own A lost object will be found, and Curtis’s What you leave behind will follow you anyway, folded away from Mei. Shinra kept performing a dinner that had lost its guest and still insisted on being delightful. Mei turned her glass and watched Celty the way she had watched Curtis, amused that the table had become territorial the moment the foreigner stood up.
“He paid for everyone and fled,” Mei said. “That’s either manners or a man who knew the next question would be worse.”
Shinra smiled too quickly.
“He’ll be back. People like that don’t leave a city once it notices them.”
Celty typed nothing. She could not eat. She could not make the fortunes smaller. She sat in a restaurant that would never be designed for her and felt both slips at once: something missing would be found, and what he left behind would follow him anyway. She had asked if it was too late to take the other night back. He had texted that she could. He had also said he already forgave her.
Those answers were following her too.

Curtis
Curtis left the tourist spot on time and immediately got into an argument with a piece of paper he was not even holding.
Long black braids swung as he walked, muttering under his breath at storefronts, crosswalks, and the entire concept of dessert.
“What you leave behind will follow you anyway. Yeah? Follow who? Anri? Celty? Rio? All three? Pick a victim, you cheap cookie. Don’t just spray prophecy on the whole contact list—”
A passing couple stared. He shut his mouth, then opened it again half a block later.
“Vague as hell. ‘Lost object will be found.’ Cool. Found where. In a doorway. In a sushi bar. In a duffle bag I’m not allowed to spend. Useless. Fortune should come with a name or a map or a refund—”
He checked the time, still walking toward Anri, still comedically furious that a cookie Celty had intercepted now had him ranking the girls in his phone like a cursed multiple-choice question. Justice, unheard, would have been on the pavement laughing until he couldn’t breathe.
Curtis was not laughing.
He was late enough to be moving and early enough to keep complaining, a tall foreigner in a Raira uniform crossing Ikebukuro with dinner behind him, a later hour ahead, and a fortune so unhelpfully accurate it had followed him out of a restaurant he had already paid to leave.
